{{also|Niet|niet-|niệt}}
==Dutch==

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|nl|dum|niwet}}, {{m|dum|niet}}, from {{inh|nl|odt|*niowiht}}, {{m|odt|niewiht}}, from {{m|odt|nio||never}} + {{m|odt|wiht||thing, creature}}. The former in turn derives from {{der|nl|gem-pro|*ne||not}} + {{m|gem-pro|*aiw-||ever}} + {{m|gem-pro|*wihtą||thing}}.

It was originally a pronoun meaning "not a thing", and was later used to reinforce a regular negation. The pronomial meaning was lost in Middle Dutch. English {{m|en|not}}, and its older forms {{m|en|naught}} and {{m|en|nought}}, were formed in the same way, but "not" also lost its sense as a pronoun and became a negation adverb as in Dutch.

